ly  to transaction fees and be completely  inflation  free so the idea here is to move to  eventually move to a pure transactional  cost system  despite the sounding a lot like you know  like the current system we're in  uh where we've got you know two people  that want to transfer money we've got a  middleman that we've got to pay  it isn't quite the same this is still  very much peer-to-peer  and the transactional cost um would  really be a flat fee so first of all  your money isn't necessarily it's not  going to  filter through a third party so to speak  it's still going peer-to-peer  but you still need that middleman to  kind of like be like almost a notary to  say hey yeah these are the two real  people this is a real transaction taking  place  also the transactional cost would most  likely  be a flat fee you wouldn't pay like a  percentage um  on the you know transaction amount it  would be a flat fee  um so really you know with bitcoin  we're talking probably one to three  dollars right to make a transfer  uh at most so right now competition for  mining and all this is extremely high  for people to acquire coins but when  coins are no longer being distributed  or if they begin to lose value the  actual hash you know difficulty is going  to drop  and so it would simply just become  easier so whether or not you have 500  000 miners or 50 000 miners  you're going to notice relatively  similar results when it comes to  transaction speed and all of that  the only threat here is that if the  number of miners was to actually drop  someone could more easily come in and  execute a 51  attack at which point the question is  would it still  not be worth um attacking the network  and we will hit this question again in a  moment but for now let's go ahead and  return to the paper  the incentive may help encourage nodes  to stay honest if a greedy attacker is  able to assemble  more cpu power than all the honest nodes  he would have to choose between using it  to defraud people by stealing back his  payments  or using it to generate new coins he  ought to find it more profitable to play  by the rules such rules that favor him  with  more new coins than everyone else  combined  than to undermine the system and  validity of his own wealth  so here's expressed why 51 attack even  if someone had the resources  doesn't really make that much sense  there's more incentive financially to  actually work with the network  um and if the incentive was just like  plain malicious to like kill the network  or something  then this would prove to be a pretty  costly endeavor  um so neither of those options are  really good  options now again this is where the  question is asked whether or not the  network will still be as secured as it  is today  by 2140 and secure as in a relative to  you know the day's mining power see here  i think it's there's a little less  certainty about security is mining  will be more about transaction fees  rather than actually acquiring coins now  keep in mind that every 10 minutes  25 bitcoin is released right now that's  worth  20 000 right every 10 minutes  so to match today's level of security or  better put  to to match today's challenge and reward  in relation to cpu power of the day  you'd need twenty thousand dollars every  ten minutes worth of transaction rewards  or transaction fees  so 120 000 an hour 2.9 million dollars a  day or just  over one billion dollars a year in  transactional  cost you know fees basically  now obviously um this is measured of  course in today's terms as well since  inflation will probably make these  numbers way bigger and to be at the same  level of security so you know as the  average computer gets faster i mean this  is all relative since breaching or  working for the network it's all going  to be in relation  to that day's current cpu standard you  know  um now that said really by 2140  so we're talking over you know a century  okay it's highly likely first of all  that there will one be a quantum  computer  it's really really unlikely that bitcoin  as we know it today will still be  trucking along by then  we're talking over a century from the  point of its development  so first off who even knows like we it's  also highly likely we'll have like  killed  each other by then but also um i don't  know any technology that hasn't  you know had to be evolved within a  century's time i mean if you just look  at you know sha-1 versus sha-256 we  already know that there are  there's security differences between the  two of them  and that's just based on the cpu growth  over time now  um i do think that all that said it's  something to consider at least though  you know that like you know consider by  2140 the question of security  but i honestly think at least my  argument is that there's no point in  thinking that far out to 2140  as by then the game is going to have  changed  significantly and we'll all be trading  like quantum coins or something like  that  so um also  i i will just there will be another  video pretty much dedicated  towards this specific uh thing as far as  how bitcoin will evolve or can bitcoin  evolve  uh over time i'll give you guys a hint  yes um  and and actually it can evolve within  the system of  bitcoin itself because bitcoin is really  one big voting scheme  um it's really one big cp one cpu one  vote  um structure and so really anything can  occur  within the realm of one cpu one vote  here so  um bitcoin can evolve and i  pretty much guarantee it um that it will  evolve by 2140. i'll bet my  my whole bank account because by then  i'll be dead anyways and so will  everyone watching this  but anyway uh that's going to conclude  the incentive tutorial video  basically the takeaway here is that  there's at least right now a large  incentive to mine  as we already know the difficulty of  mining is systematic and it changes  based on the amount of miners mining  so it's always going to be the case at  least until 2140  that it's going to make fiscal sense  more fiscal sense to mine for the  network  than to attack the network especially  because if you attack the network  you're going to like first of all it's  going to be very costly  and versus if you work for the network  we're talking you know if you let's say  you had over 50  of the network well that means every 10  minutes you're going to get a 50 50  chance of getting those coins  so we're talking every 10 minutes well  every hour you would make 60 000 every  hour  you know with your 50 50 odds so  uh i would think that it would be in  your favor to go ahead and work for the  network  so um so that's it if you have any  questions or comments feel free to leave  them below  as always thanks for watching thanks for  all the support in the subscriptions and  until  the next video

